# Derived content — paths regenerated from a source of record, never authored.
#
# `merge=rwv-ours` names the resolution; it does not carry it. The definition
# does not travel with a clone — rwv supplies it per operation, as a
# `DerivedContentPolicy` stated at the Vcs seam — so a replay rwv drives keeps
# the target side of these paths instead of stopping on a conflict that has no
# right answer to adjudicate.
#
# Nothing regenerates during the merge. The side that survives is deliberately
# stale with respect to the merged sources, and `scripts/ci-local.sh`'s final
# stage — regenerate, then diff the working tree against the index — is what
# forces the regeneration commit before anything ships.
#
# CAVEAT: git invoked by hand has no definition for this driver. rwv plants a
# durable `merge.rwv-ours.driver` config in project repos only, and this repo
# is not one, so a merge or rebase run outside rwv here falls back to an
# ordinary textual conflict. `rwv.lock` has always carried the same caveat.
# Resolve such a conflict either way and re-run the generator — the drift gate
# catches the result on both routes.
#
# `templates/` is the source of record for the pages generated beside it, so it
# is authored content and is deliberately left out of the declaration: a
# side-pick there would discard someone's edit with nothing to regenerate it
# from.

docs/reference/explain/**      merge=rwv-ours
docs/reference/schemas/**      merge=rwv-ours
docs/reference/prime/**        merge=rwv-ours
docs/reference/*/templates/**  !merge

# Every text file checks out LF on every host, whatever its core.autocrlf or
# core.eol says; Git for Windows installs core.autocrlf=true. This repository's
# bytes are read as well as compiled: the tests scan them, include_str! embeds
# the docs in the binary, and a POSIX shell reads a CR after a shebang as part
# of the interpreter's name. A path that must hold CRLF is named below with
# -text, and tests/repo_line_endings_test.rs holds the rule and that list.
* text=auto eol=lf

# gita writes its CSVs with CRLF line endings, Python's csv default, and these
# fixtures are the bytes it writes.
tests/fixtures/gita/*.csv -text
